.TH CLOVER 1 "September 2026" "@clover-cli/cli" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clover \- inspect and manage your cloud accounts from the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clover aws login
.RB [ \-\-access\-key\-id
.IR id ]
.RB [ \-\-secret\-access\-key
.IR secret ]
.RB [ \-\-session\-token
.IR token ]
.RB [ \-\-region
.IR region ]
.br
.B clover aws whoami
.br
.B clover aws list\-resources
.br
.B clover aws logout
.br
.B clover aws
.RB { ec2 | rds | dynamodb | s3 | lambda }
.I action
.RI [ name ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B clover
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clover
connects to your cloud provider and shows what is running in your account.
AWS is currently the only supported provider.
.PP
Credentials are read from the standard AWS environment variables (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
the same ones used by
.BR aws (1)
and the AWS SDKs.
.B clover
never writes credentials to disk.
.PP
Read access is enough to inspect the account; the AWS managed policy
.B ReadOnlyAccess
covers it.
Creating, changing and deleting resources needs write access to each
service used.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B aws login
Verify AWS credentials and print the shell commands that export them.
Any value not given as an option is asked for interactively; the secret
access key is not echoed.
The credentials are checked with STS
.IR GetCallerIdentity ,
which needs no IAM permissions.
.IP
On success, the
.B export
commands are printed to standard output and everything else (prompts and
status messages) to standard error, so the output can be passed to
.BR eval .
When no session token is given,
.B unset AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
is printed instead, so a leftover token does not break the new keys.
Nothing is printed to standard output if verification fails.
See
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B aws whoami
Check that the credentials in the environment work, and print the ARN,
account ID and region they belong to.
.TP
.B aws list\-resources
List the resources in the account, grouped by service with a count each.
EC2 instances, RDS instances, DynamoDB tables, S3 buckets and Lambda
functions are listed directly.
Other services are found through the Resource Groups Tagging API, which only
returns resources that have (or once had) tags.
Everything except S3 is limited to the configured region; S3 buckets are
listed for the whole account.
.IP
If one service cannot be listed (for example, because of a missing IAM
permission), its row shows the error and the other services are still listed.
.TP
.B aws logout
Print the shell command that removes the AWS credentials from the
environment:
.RS
.PP
.EX
unset AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY AWS_SESSION_TOKEN AWS_REGION
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI "aws ec2|rds|dynamodb|s3|lambda " action
Create, list, show, update and delete resources in that service:
EC2 instances, RDS database instances, DynamoDB tables and items, S3 buckets
and objects, and Lambda functions.
Every service has the actions
.BR create ,
.BR list ,
.BR get ,
.B update
and
.BR delete ;
some have more (for example
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR invoke ,
.BR put\-item ,
.BR upload ).
Each accepts
.BI \-\-region " region"\fR,
.B \-\-output table\fR|\fBjson
and
.BI \-\-config " file.json"\fR,
which reads any option from a JSON file so a whole setup runs as one command.
Deletes ask for confirmation unless
.B \-\-yes
is given, and require it when not run in a terminal.
Run any action with
.B \-\-help
for its options, or see
.I docs/aws.md
in the source repository.
.TP
.B completion
Print a bash/zsh completion script.
Append it to
.I ~/.bashrc
or
.IR ~/.zshrc .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "aws login"
.TP
.BI \-\-access\-key\-id " id"
AWS access key ID.
.TP
.BI \-\-secret\-access\-key " secret"
AWS secret access key.
Passing it as an option leaves it in your shell history; leave it out to be
prompted instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-session\-token " token"
Session token, only needed for temporary credentials.
Never prompted for.
.TP
.BI \-\-region " region"
Region to use, for example
.BR eu\-west\-1 .
Defaults to
.B us\-east\-1
when left empty at the prompt.
.SS "All commands"
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help for the command and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version number and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
Access key ID.
Required.
.TP
.B AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
Secret access key.
Required.
.TP
.B AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
Session token, for temporary credentials.
Optional.
.TP
.B AWS_REGION
Region to use.
Optional.
.TP
.B AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
Used when
.B AWS_REGION
is not set.
If neither is set, the region is
.BR us\-east\-1 .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or rejected credentials, a failed request to AWS, or invalid
command-line usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Log in interactively and load the credentials into the current shell:
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(clover aws login)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Log in with a given key and region, prompting only for the secret:
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(clover aws login \-\-access\-key\-id AKIA... \-\-region us\-west\-2)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the credentials, then list what is in the account:
.PP
.RS
.EX
clover aws whoami
clover aws list\-resources
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use credentials set some other way, without logging in:
.PP
.RS
.EX
export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIA...
export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
clover aws whoami
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove the credentials from the current shell:
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(clover aws logout)"
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
A program cannot change the environment of the shell that started it.
That is why
.B aws login
and
.B aws logout
print shell commands instead of setting the variables themselves, and why
they are meant to be wrapped in
.BR eval .
Running them without
.B eval
only prints the commands.
.PP
The
.B eval
form works in POSIX shells such as
.BR bash (1)
and
.BR zsh (1).
In other shells, set the printed variables by hand.
.PP
To have the credentials in every new shell, export them from your shell
startup file (for example
.IR ~/.zshrc )
or from a per-project
.I .env
file loaded by a tool such as
.BR direnv (1).
Never commit a file that contains keys.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aws (1),
.BR eval (1p)
.PP
Documentation: https://github.com/clover-cli/cli/tree/main/docs
